💬 Done — **plan.md item 1 is closed: the platform is renamed Udaan → Thok.**

## What I did

**2,091 occurrences across 846 files**, tracked as git renames for the three `udaan-backup` units. The board's own counts reproduced exactly before I started, so the plan was measured rather than guessed.

The name wasn't a guess either: `Thok` was **already in the tree** — the source design is `Thok Wholesale App` and `Thok Credit` is the credit product's name in live bilingual copy. So the rename direction had no collision, and the platform now shares a name with its credit line instead of disagreeing with it.

**Three things a `sed` could not have done:**

1. **The desk rail's mark was the letter `U`** — the wordmark's first letter, which isn't the string "udaan" and which no sweep for the old name can see. It's `T` now. This is the one place the code deliberately contradicts `design.html`, which is a read-only input that predates the rename and still draws `U`; I recorded the deviation at the declaration and in CLAUDE.md, on the rule already used twice there for AA contrast and target size.

2. **Four strings held back**, each naming a third party or a read-only input rather than this product: `udaan.com` (×10, the real company's site, in historical prose about the canonical bug — renaming it would describe a bug that never happened), `@udaan_india` (×5, that company's Twitter handle), `msbadar/udaan` (×1, the actual git remote), and `design.html`'s single `Udaan`. **The platform was renamed; the company was not** — that's plan.md item 3, still open, and I deliberately didn't answer it by way of a rename.

3. **I got the exclusion list half right first.** I carried `--exclude=design.html` into the rename command but dropped the `--exclude=plan.md` the measurement command had, so the board renamed itself into "Rename the app from Thok to Thok". Caught, reverted, and written into the closed row — the two commands differed by one flag, both looked correct, and the file counts coincided at 846 because swapping one excluded file for another is still 846.

## Verification

A rename touching every import isn't the "small work" the board's working agreement exempts, so I ran all six gates rather than carrying them:

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| typecheck | pass (8 workspaces + `tools/render`) |
| lint | pass |
| `npm test` | **exit 0** — api 1815/117, admin 835/55, web 442/33, mobile 1493, landing 235/16 |
| build | pass |
| `prettier --check` | pass (4 files needed reflow — `Thok` is a character shorter) |
| `npm run openapi` | regenerates to exactly one changed line, `"title": "Thok API"` — drift check clean |

**Every per-workspace test count is identical to its recorded baseline**, which is the result that matters: a rename that moved behaviour would move a count.

## Two things to know

- **Unrun:** no migration has ever been applied here, so the renamed Postgres role and database exist nowhere yet. And I did **not** re-run `tools/render` — the rename changed one drawn glyph and no layout rule, which is precisely the "this moves nothing" claim CLAUDE.md says to render anyway. Worth a `tools/render/run.sh admin` when convenient.
- **Timing was the whole saving.** Five of the eleven rows have consequences landing outside the repo; all five were free *only* because nothing is deployed and nobody is signed in. That window closes at the first real deploy.

I've updated CLAUDE.md (Part I gained a section on the four carve-outs; § "State of the world" and § "The clients" gained the rename) and plan.md (item 1 → Closed, with items 2–4 **deliberately not renumbered**, since CLAUDE.md now cites "plan.md item 3"). I haven't committed — say the word and I will.

