## What I did

The board is almost entirely blocked on owner decisions. Exactly one item was marked decided and mechanical — item 4's react group (*"the only mechanical one. Merge it."*). I merged it. **It was not mechanical**, and that's the finding worth your attention.

**`react` 19.2.8 → 19.3.0 fails 77 of `apps/mobile`'s 89 suites**, every one at import. `@testing-library/react-native` hard-checks in `ensurePeerDeps` that `react-test-renderer` equals `react` *exactly* — not a peer-range warning, a refusal to load. Dependabot's react group is configured by package name and doesn't contain `react-test-renderer`, so it bumped one and not the other. The merge therefore carries a line Dependabot did not write: `apps/mobile`'s `react-test-renderer` → `^19.3.0`.

This is item 4's own lesson one layer down. That row was already corrected once for reading branch *names* instead of version numbers; here the version numbers really are a minor, and reading them still wasn't enough. **A grouped bump has to be read against the packages that *constrain* the group, not only the ones in it** — a different check from the `git diff … package.json` the row learned, and no reading of the diff would have found it.

Two other things, both recorded:

- **The lockfile needed `npm dedupe`.** `npm install` is minimal-change and won't re-hoist, so it left root `react` at 19.2.8 and gave each of five workspaces a nested 19.3.0 — two React instances in one bundle, invisible in a manifest diff. CLAUDE.md now carries the topology check rather than relying on the diff.
- **Dedupe's cost, stated rather than hidden:** it consolidated 26 unrelated dev-tooling transitives, including five downgrades to the lowest version satisfying every declared range. All are `devDependency` transitives of the schematics/expo-cli/webpack/eslint trees; none is a runtime dependency of any shipped image. The manifest diff is react-only.

The rename conflict the board predicted landed exactly as described, in the three files it named.

**Verified:** 5504 tests / 359 suites — every workspace count identical to CLAUDE.md § "Tests" — plus typecheck, lint and `prettier --check`. I did not run builds or the render harness; no layout, token or stylesheet changed. Node here is v20 against the repo's pinned 22.23.2, so `npm install` warned `EBADENGINE` throughout; resolution isn't node-version-dependent, but CI on 22.11/24 is the real check.

## What I deliberately did not do

The remaining four Dependabot branches are four separate decisions of different weight, and the board is explicit that CI can't stand in for them (`ci.yml` has never run on a push). Each needs your call:

- **`ioredis` 5 → 6** — runtime major on the rate-limit store; blast radius is production-only now that `RATE_LIMIT_ALLOW_IN_PROCESS` exists.
- **nest 11 → 12** and **prisma 6 → 7** — two framework majors, not the same commit. Prisma's is heavier: migrations are checked in and the generated client reaches all 117 API specs through `test/prisma-double.ts`.
- **tooling** — seven majors including Vitest 3 → 5 and Jest 29 → 30, the two runners every coverage floor is expressed against. The board says split rather than merge, and I'd agree: expect the same dedupe trap, since the bumped packages *are* the runners.
- **un-pausing CodeQL/Dependabot** — a yes/no; un-pausing is also what stops the four branches going stale.

Item 6 (merging `work` into `development`) I left alone — it's outward-facing and there's no `ssh` here to push anyway. Items 2 and 3 are blocked on a design direction and five product decisions respectively; settings (A)–(G) are outside this sandbox. One note: `git rev-list --count origin/development..HEAD` now returns **14**, and because the board made that a command rather than a number last revision, nothing went stale — the fix paid off immediately.

