## What I did

Re-measured every claim on the board rather than carrying it forward (its stated convention), and found two things wrong.

**Finding 1 — the one claim nobody had ever re-measured.** Item 4 closed its branch table with *"they sit at the versions they were cut at and go stale in place."* Every other line on the board is re-asked each revision; that one was carried verbatim, because it reads as a process description rather than a measurement. Asked of the registry package by package, **none of the three outstanding branches is stale** — all 21 packages resolve to the highest stable version:

| Branch | Result |
|---|---|
| `nest` | `^12.0.1`/`^12.0.0` — carets admit 12.0.3, 12.0.2, 12.0.1, 12.0.0 |
| `prisma` | `^7.10.0` — exact match to highest stable |
| `tooling` | eight pins are `latest` outright; the two `^5.0.0`s admit 5.0.1 |

It's a mechanism written as an observation. The mechanism is real (a paused Dependabot rebases nothing) but hasn't fired — the branches are three days old. This costs the *reason* rather than the conclusion: urgency comes from each branch carrying a migration or a support-policy change, measured separately.

**Finding 2 — precision lost on the way into summaries.** Item 4's `tooling` bullet recorded the `@eslint/js` peer correctly all along ("10.0.1 having gained an `eslint ^10.0.0` peer"). Both tables summarising it had compressed to the major. That doesn't survive measurement: `@eslint/js@10.0.0` declares no `peerDependencies` at all, like the 9.36.0 it replaces — the edge exists only because the branch pins `^10.0.1`. Also, `@eslint/js` doesn't track `eslint`: latest is 10.0.1 against eslint's 10.10.0.

## Worth flagging

I published a shell command for the currency check and **tested it rather than just writing it** — the naive form (`npm view "$p" version`) reads the `latest` dist-tag and reports `prisma ^7.10.0 -> 8.0.0-rc.15`, calling a current stable pin stale by a major. That is exactly the trap this board documented two revisions ago, and I'd have committed it inside the paragraph that cites it. The published version asks what the range resolves to and what the highest non-prerelease is; the table is its verified output.

